+/*
+ * Galaxy Note 10 5G (d1x, SM-N971N, Exynos 9825): the vibration motor.
+ * Milestone M1 of research/AUDIO_HAPTICS_9825.md: the motor buzzes once at
+ * boot when a boot-arg asks for it. Nothing here runs unless d2shaptic >= 1.
+ *
+ * The motor is an LRA driven by a Cirrus Logic CS40L25A (DEVID 0x40A25A) at
+ * I2C address 0x40. Written from facts, no code copied:
+ *  - d1x DT (exynos9820-d1x_kor_23.dts:8243, exynos9825.dts): cs40l25@40 on
+ *    hsi2c_44 (pins gpm10-0 SCL / gpm11-0 SDA), reset-gpios gpg1-7 (high =
+ *    running; pinctrl "motor-boost-en" drives it high), interrupt gph1-0,
+ *    boost 1000 nH / 4 uF / 4000 mA, gpio1-mode 1, amp-gnd-stby. No supply
+ *    phandles: VA (1.8 V) and VP (battery) are always on, so no ACPM/S2MPS19
+ *    rail is touched here.
+ *  - Samsung's S-Boot of this phone (N971NKSU2HXJ2 sboot.bin, disassembled
+ *    offline): register_cs40l2x_vib/cs40l2x_vib_enable pulse gpg1-7 (GPIO
+ *    0x647 = bank 50 = 0x104300a0, pin 7) low 2 ms, high, wait 1 ms, then
+ *    over a GPIO bit-banged I2C ("EXT_I2C" bus 14 = GPIO 0x200 SCL / 0x220
+ *    SDA = gpm10-0 / gpm11-0) write BASIC_BUZZ_FREQ 0x0280017c = 158,
+ *    BASIC_BUZZ_LEVEL 0x02800180 = 130, BASIC_BUZZ_DURATION 0x02800184 = 25,
+ *    read each back, log "%s freq. %dHz %dVrms %dms", and buzz with
+ *    BASIC_BUZZ_TRIGGER 0x02800188 = 1, then waits 100 (its delay helper
+ *    0xd2af4; ms assumed). That is the ROM ("basic mode") buzz, run on every
+ *    cold boot: no firmware needed. Code at sboot.bin offsets 0xceb00-0xcece4
+ *    (vib), 0xc6280 (I2C write), 0xa62cc (bus 14 pins).
+ *  - Samsung's Linux driver (CruelKernel cruel-HVJ5-v5.3 drivers/motor/
+ *    cs40l2x.c, read for facts only): probe order, basic-mode exit, B0
+ *    errata, boost and routing, wmfw/wmdr block placement, DSP start.
+ *  - The phone's cs40l25a.wmfw (38224 bytes, crc32 0x90661b70, FW ID
+ *    0x1400c3 rev 8.8.1) decoded offline with tools/ah_tools/wmfw_syms.py:
+ *    the XM addresses of its controls below hold only for that exact file,
+ *    so level 3 refuses any other file.
+ *
+ * Bus: GPIO bit-bang on gpm10-0/gpm11-0, the way S-Boot talks to this chip.
+ * The HSI2C helpers of a5xelte_touch.c (patch 0130 timing) are not reused:
+ * their timing assumes the 26 MHz OSCCLK the loader sets for hsi2c_10,
+ * while I2C_CMGP2's clock mux (CMU_CMGP 0x15c0100c) picks OSCCLK_RCO_CMGP or
+ * the CMGP bus and nobody sets it up under iOS. Open drain is emulated
+ * (output-low or input), so both lines need the board's pull-ups, which the
+ * DT implies (hsi2c44-bus pin-pud 0). At the default 2 us half clock one
+ * register access takes ~0.5 ms and the whole firmware ~2 s (plus as long
+ * again to read it back); this thread spins meanwhile, sleeping 2 ms after
+ * every 240-byte burst so the boot keeps going.
+ *
+ * Boot-args:
+ *   d2shaptic=0      default: nothing at all (no MMIO, no pins touched)
+ *   d2shaptic=1      look: pins, chip as S-Boot left it, reset pulse, OTP
+ *                    boot, DEVID/REVID, ROM (basic mode) state. No drive.
+ *   d2shaptic=2      1 + the ROM buzz: S-Boot's frequency and level, for
+ *                    d2shaptic_ms (default 150, 10..300)
+ *   d2shaptic=3      1 + firmware: basic-mode exit, errata, boost, routing,
+ *                    cs40l25a.wmfw + cs40l20.bin loaded and read back, DSP
+ *                    started; one click (wave d2shaptic_idx, default 1, 0 =
+ *                    none) and one buzz of d2shaptic_ms. No ROM buzz here, so
+ *                    what is felt comes from the firmware path.
+ *   d2shaptic_level  ROM buzz level, 1..130 (S-Boot's 130 is the cap)
+ *   d2shaptic_us     I2C half period in us (default 2, 1..50)
+ *   d2shaptic_verify=0  skip the firmware read-back (halves level 3's time)
+ * Firmware for level 3: the boot image's D2SFiles blob (patch 0133) is
+ * searched for paths ending in /cs40l25a.wmfw and /cs40l20.bin; failing
+ * that, /usr/share/firmware/<name> is read once the root is mounted.
+ *
+ * Limits, whatever is asked: at most one ROM buzz or one click and one buzz
+ * per boot; a buzz is at most 300 ms, and the chip is told so itself (ROM
+ * DURATION, firmware TIMEOUT_MS), so it stops even if this thread or the
+ * kernel dies; this thread also ends it (ENDPLAYBACK) and forces the chip
+ * off (reset low) 100 ms after the expected end. Every wait is bounded. Any
+ * fault (no ACK, wrong ID, read-back mismatch, ROM or amp error bit, boost
+ * short) stops the run. At the end of every run the chip is held in reset,
+ * which also turns its boost converter off, and the I2C pins are released.
+ * Log lines start with "D1X HAPTIC:".
+ */
+#include <sys/param.h>
+#include <sys/systm.h>
+#include <sys/fcntl.h>
